WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.041 --> 00:00:01.751
So over the past couple of weeks,

00:00:01.751 --> 00:00:05.588
some of you have directed me
to this very strange project.

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:09.217
It's a city that's 170 kilometers long,

00:00:09.634 --> 00:00:13.430
500 meters tall, and it's basically a line

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:17.684
cutting straight across a desert
with a facade made of mirrors.

00:00:18.309 --> 00:00:23.106
And you can travel from one end
to the other end in just 20 minutes.

00:00:23.106 --> 00:00:26.109
It's got a ultra
super high speed public transit,

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:30.405
which eliminates
the need for cars and prioritizes walking.

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:34.492
I first I thought it was a metaverse
project, but it is a real project.

00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:40.373
The initial estimates for
this project was $500 billion

00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:45.086
and now they're estimating at $1 trillion

00:00:45.503 --> 00:00:48.840
and it's expected to house 9 million
residents.

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:51.176
There's been a lot of marketing
around this.

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:55.513
So let's unpack this project
a little bit from the design and

00:00:55.513 --> 00:00:58.600
urban planning point of view.

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.770
So a little bit of context.

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.856
Neom is a part of the Saudi government's

00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:09.569
bigger initiative
to diversify the economy away from oil.

00:01:09.569 --> 00:01:13.031
And as a part of their plan,
they're investing heavily

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:17.118
into infrastructure projects
as well as education,

00:01:17.118 --> 00:01:21.039
tourism,
research, age, anti-aging, technology.

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:25.877
And there's a really big focus
on future industries like renewable power.

00:01:25.877 --> 00:01:28.755
And the Saudi Arabian government
set up an entire zone

00:01:28.838 --> 00:01:32.884
for the Neom development
in an area called Tabuk.

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:37.305
There's areas that's already finished
construction, including a seaside

00:01:37.305 --> 00:01:42.435
resort, a massive development
for the royal family, and an airport

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:46.272
directly connecting to Riyadh,
which is the capital of Saudi

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:48.733
Arabia.

00:01:49.484 --> 00:01:52.779
The line is probably the most public

00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:56.449
and heavily market
a part of this neom developments.

00:01:56.491 --> 00:02:02.163
The graphics to date have been
really beautiful and really compelling.

00:02:02.205 --> 00:02:04.749
According to Mohammed bin Salman.

00:02:05.083 --> 00:02:08.711
This concept has a bunch of advantages
that's going to make this

00:02:08.711 --> 00:02:10.296
the city of the future.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:13.216
By the way,
it's going to have zero carbon emissions

00:02:13.216 --> 00:02:15.760
and it's going to run on clean,
renewable energy.

00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:19.264
The city is going to be structured
on three different levels.

00:02:19.389 --> 00:02:22.267
At ground level,
you're going to have your pedestrian zone.

00:02:22.392 --> 00:02:24.894
There's going to be no roads and no cars.

00:02:24.894 --> 00:02:28.356
And there's going to be tons of parks
and trees to encourage walking.

00:02:28.481 --> 00:02:31.442
The second level functions
as a service level

00:02:31.693 --> 00:02:35.405
with shops and other commercial spaces,
and on the third level,

00:02:35.446 --> 00:02:39.742
you got your goods and transportation
with this ultra high speed transit system.

00:02:39.784 --> 00:02:43.788
This connects the different city modules
so that all of your activities

00:02:43.788 --> 00:02:48.168
can be done by public transport
or by walking and all of your daily

00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:52.213
needs can be met pretty much
within a five minute walking distance.

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:56.467
This whole idea is built on the premise
that most big cities

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:59.596
are born out of the Industrial Revolution,

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:04.184
and they were built for machines and cars
and industry rather than for people.

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:08.646
The line would save humanity
from this commuting nightmare,

00:03:08.646 --> 00:03:12.609
but is a rigid line
really going to meet people's needs?

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:17.155
And is this really the most
conducive shape for a city?

00:03:19.991 --> 00:03:22.660
There's just a lot of questions
that come up when I look at this.

00:03:22.785 --> 00:03:26.623
The first one was the design's impact
on the natural habitat.

00:03:26.664 --> 00:03:31.878
I mean, if the focus really is on
green technology and its design

00:03:31.878 --> 00:03:36.174
for sustainability, why does it literally
cut through the surroundings?

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:38.593
You know,
it looks like it's forcing its way

00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:42.305
into this irregular terrain
and it doesn't even take advantage

00:03:42.305 --> 00:03:46.142
of the coast, which to me
seems like the most natural thing to do.

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:50.230
I studied fragmented landscapes
and wildlife corridors in architecture

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:54.150
school when I was doing my project
in the Korean Demilitarized Zone.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:57.904
And there's thousands of instances
throughout the world where

00:03:58.071 --> 00:04:02.408
natural habitats have been fragmented
from roads or buildings.

00:04:02.742 --> 00:04:04.827
It stops animal migration.

00:04:04.827 --> 00:04:06.996
It kills off weaker animal species.

00:04:06.996 --> 00:04:09.165
It limits genetic diversity.

00:04:09.165 --> 00:04:12.752
It creates inbreeding,
which creates genetic disorders.

00:04:12.794 --> 00:04:14.879
This is really just to name a few.

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:17.882
And one of the things I humans
can do to help

00:04:17.882 --> 00:04:21.928
these situations
is to create these wildlife corridors.

00:04:22.053 --> 00:04:23.096
And honestly,

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:27.725
I don't know if this was overlooked
because the project is in the desert,

00:04:27.725 --> 00:04:33.314
but even within a desert, there's
thousands of animals and plant species

00:04:33.314 --> 00:04:38.945
like lizards, snakes, turtles, sand,
cats, hares, like literally thousands.

00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.948
And I would assume, especially as you get

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:44.742
closer to the water,
you would have more animal species.

00:04:45.034 --> 00:04:47.578
So creating this massive

00:04:47.954 --> 00:04:53.334
170 kilometer wall,
it sounds really problematic.

00:04:53.376 --> 00:04:57.297
Also, this giant mirror of wall
is going to reflect the light

00:04:57.297 --> 00:05:01.634
and it's going to create these two really,
really hot heat zones.

00:05:01.884 --> 00:05:04.220
And it's
going to fry everything around it.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:06.514
Not to mention birds flying into it.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:09.267
The second thing is transportation.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:13.187
How does this extremely long line

00:05:13.396 --> 00:05:17.066
cut transit time
compare to a rectangle or a circle?

00:05:17.108 --> 00:05:21.279
The proposal relies on the super fast
transit system

00:05:21.279 --> 00:05:25.742
that's going to take you from
one end to the other in just 20 minutes.

00:05:26.034 --> 00:05:32.415
That would mean that
the train runs at 525 kilometers per hour.

00:05:32.415 --> 00:05:36.294
If you're not accounting
for all the stops, the fastest train in

00:05:36.294 --> 00:05:41.132
the world is the Shanghai Maglev,
which runs at 460 kilometers per hour.

00:05:41.257 --> 00:05:45.094
There's also a successful examples
of how transit has been incorporated

00:05:45.094 --> 00:05:49.724
into the streetscape at a fraction
of the cost of these bullet trains.

00:05:49.766 --> 00:05:53.478
I feel like this design
and this concept of the line is creating

00:05:53.728 --> 00:05:58.816
unnecessary issues and trying to solve
that issue with expensive technology.

00:05:58.858 --> 00:06:03.237
And I think design should provide
the best organic solution,

00:06:03.237 --> 00:06:06.366
and we should use technology
to improve on that.

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:11.746
Also, organic cities, they grow.

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:16.042
You know, how is this line
going to allow for growth?

00:06:16.084 --> 00:06:20.671
Do you just keep extending the line
like adding more modules

00:06:20.922 --> 00:06:25.635
or do you add perpendicularly, in
which case it wouldn't be a line anymore?

00:06:25.802 --> 00:06:28.971
Also, if you look at studies on innovation
throughout history,

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:32.350
like where good ideas come from
by Steven Johnson.

00:06:32.475 --> 00:06:34.602
He says by innovation.

00:06:34.602 --> 00:06:37.605
Grew exactly when cities started to grow.

00:06:37.605 --> 00:06:38.773
And that's because there's

00:06:38.773 --> 00:06:43.277
a lot of cross-pollination
and accidental meeting of ideas.

00:06:43.361 --> 00:06:46.406
He describes
the different environmental examples

00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:50.284
that breed innovation
and every single instance.

00:06:50.284 --> 00:06:54.288
It's more of a maze
than a straight forward line.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:59.168
So even if the city has been programed
so that you can access your grocery store

00:06:59.168 --> 00:07:03.214
or your local coffee shop
within a five minute walking distance,

00:07:03.214 --> 00:07:07.009
if they're spending
all of this money on it, I'm assuming that

00:07:07.009 --> 00:07:09.929
they're going to want innovators
and leaders to come live here.

00:07:10.012 --> 00:07:13.599
But a linear, rigid city
just doesn't really seem

00:07:13.599 --> 00:07:17.145
like the right kind of environment
for the breeding of ideas.

00:07:17.186 --> 00:07:20.356
There's also the pure logistics of this
as well.

00:07:20.481 --> 00:07:21.649
For example, let's

00:07:21.649 --> 00:07:25.653
say you're making some mac and cheese,
but you realize you don't have any butter

00:07:25.653 --> 00:07:28.739
and how can you make any mac and cheese
without any butter?

00:07:28.823 --> 00:07:32.452
And so you decide to quickly
go to the grocery store

00:07:32.785 --> 00:07:35.705
and if you want to do it
fast, you have to walk, which

00:07:35.872 --> 00:07:37.790
with this type of verticality,

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:40.084
you have to take the stairs
if you want to do it quick.

00:07:40.209 --> 00:07:43.588
But if you have mobility issues,
you're forced

00:07:43.629 --> 00:07:47.592
to take the elevator,
which we all know is a little bit slow.

00:07:47.717 --> 00:07:52.680
And as we've seen from the pandemic, it's
the perfect breeding ground for disease.

00:07:52.722 --> 00:07:56.017
There's some talk of, again,
another high tech

00:07:56.017 --> 00:07:59.979
vertical transportation system,
which I'm sure will help

00:08:00.146 --> 00:08:05.193
revolutionize transportation
in a lot of already densities.

00:08:05.526 --> 00:08:09.447
But putting these constraints
at the outset of the design

00:08:09.697 --> 00:08:14.827
to serve this very rigid concept,
I don't know how sensible that is also.

00:08:15.286 --> 00:08:17.079
Do you think we're going to be able
to cycle here?

00:08:17.079 --> 00:08:21.334
We all know cycling to your daily commute
increases health, lower

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:22.585
stress and obesity.

00:08:22.585 --> 00:08:25.755
But you need roads of some size, which

00:08:25.755 --> 00:08:28.674
this design doesn't
really seem to allow for.

00:08:28.841 --> 00:08:32.053
So do you need to go to the basement floor
to ride your bike?

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:33.679
I don't know.

00:08:33.679 --> 00:08:37.725
I'm also not super sure how much daylight
you're going to be able to get in there.

00:08:37.808 --> 00:08:40.645
This gray box is residential,

00:08:40.978 --> 00:08:44.440
but if you live all the way over here,

00:08:44.607 --> 00:08:47.860
that's a pretty unpleasant situation
for you.

00:08:47.860 --> 00:08:50.279
And you're not going to be able
to get a lot of daylight.

00:08:50.446 --> 00:08:54.325
So maybe the ones on the lower
level are the servants and the workers.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:58.621
And up here, that's
where all the millionaires live.

00:08:58.913 --> 00:09:01.290
If this gets built,
I'm sure they're going to figure out a way

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:04.126
to properly ventilate
and structurally support this.

00:09:04.168 --> 00:09:07.213
But I think it's going to come
at a serious cost.

00:09:07.296 --> 00:09:10.716
Every structural engineer
is going to tell you that putting up

00:09:10.716 --> 00:09:16.180
a massive slab of wall is going to create
some serious lateral stability issues.

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:19.976
So that means all of this is going to need
to have some beefy structure.

00:09:20.184 --> 00:09:23.229
This is the classic thermal convection
diagram.

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:27.900
The desert gets up to 55 degrees Celsius.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:30.403
That's 130 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:09:30.444 --> 00:09:34.365
So if the roof of this thing stays open,

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:38.411
like in the renderings, you're going
to need some serious cooling in there.

00:09:38.494 --> 00:09:43.666
Or maybe they're just counting on all of
these areas being shaded most of the time.

00:09:43.791 --> 00:09:48.879
But then I'm kind of curious,
how do you get all of this lush greenery?

00:09:49.088 --> 00:09:49.755
Right.

00:09:49.755 --> 00:09:52.508
These are only the handful

00:09:52.508 --> 00:09:55.553
of the many issues with this concept.

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:58.723
I feel like a lot of unnecessary
constraints

00:09:58.723 --> 00:10:02.268
have been created
to serve this very simple concept.

00:10:02.351 --> 00:10:05.396
It reminds me of like an architecture
student project

00:10:05.688 --> 00:10:08.608
that inevitably gets ripped apart
during a grid.

00:10:08.774 --> 00:10:14.405
But let's see that they're able to resolve
the issue of daylight, of walkability.

00:10:14.655 --> 00:10:20.119
They can make trees
levitate, and sunlight can bend geometry.

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:24.498
But in the end, the question is still
who is going to live here?

00:10:24.707 --> 00:10:27.251
You know, what kind of communities
are they going to have?

00:10:27.376 --> 00:10:30.671
This is a $1 trillion project

00:10:31.505 --> 00:10:35.801
and they're estimating that it's
going to cost even more

00:10:35.843 --> 00:10:39.013
because all of these technologies
still have to be developed

00:10:39.305 --> 00:10:42.767
and they're really trying
to get the state of art for everything.

00:10:42.975 --> 00:10:46.896
The Saudi government is obviously
not going to foot the bill for everything.

00:10:46.979 --> 00:10:51.734
You know, that's partially the whole point
of this entire marketing campaign.

00:10:51.734 --> 00:10:56.322
It's to attract investors
in typical developments of this size.

00:10:56.322 --> 00:10:59.659
They're going to phase it
so that the profits from the first phase

00:10:59.659 --> 00:11:04.038
confront the second phase and the second
to the third and so on and so forth.

00:11:04.121 --> 00:11:10.002
But that would lead to old and new
sections and with typical master plans.

00:11:10.002 --> 00:11:13.422
These sections are designed
very strategically

00:11:13.756 --> 00:11:15.549
so that the first phase, you know,

00:11:15.549 --> 00:11:19.679
even if it's a standalone,
can be a livable and pleasant community.

00:11:19.845 --> 00:11:23.724
But with just a couple
of strips of the line,

00:11:23.808 --> 00:11:26.310
which is the old section
and which are the new section,

00:11:26.644 --> 00:11:30.189
and cannot even function
as a strip on its own.

00:11:30.356 --> 00:11:33.484
The idea of a massive city
is really cool, but

00:11:33.484 --> 00:11:36.654
who's going to want to move in there
when it's just one little section?

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:41.075
The Saudi government really
wants to set up Neom as a free trade zone

00:11:41.158 --> 00:11:44.078
with its own tax system and legal systems.

00:11:44.120 --> 00:11:48.833
That's separate from Saudi Arabia's
super conservative legal system.

00:11:48.833 --> 00:11:52.545
And they're really trying to create
an environment for global investors.

00:11:52.712 --> 00:11:56.674
So maybe it'll be a trade area
where people come and go,

00:11:56.966 --> 00:11:59.969
but maybe few
people will actually live there.

00:12:00.177 --> 00:12:03.222
They're also proposing
that AI is going to help us live

00:12:03.222 --> 00:12:08.018
more efficiently,
reduce waste and live more comfortably.

00:12:08.018 --> 00:12:11.355
But people are already skittish

00:12:11.355 --> 00:12:15.359
about giving up their data,
even for the most utility sharing needs.

00:12:15.401 --> 00:12:19.447
And so to expect people
to do that for a city that's built

00:12:19.447 --> 00:12:23.367
and funded
by an authoritarian government is,

00:12:23.492 --> 00:12:27.121
I think, asking for a lot.

00:12:27.329 --> 00:12:32.334
I have no doubt that this project is going
to push the boundaries of technology.

00:12:32.626 --> 00:12:36.005
They're already making some really,
really interesting advancements

00:12:36.005 --> 00:12:37.965
in desalination technology.

00:12:37.965 --> 00:12:40.551
But building a city from scratch

00:12:40.843 --> 00:12:43.471
instead of solving the existing problems

00:12:43.846 --> 00:12:48.642
is, in my opinion,
the antidote assist of sustainability.

00:12:48.768 --> 00:12:52.897
Many parts of Saudi Arabia
don't even have a proper sewer system.

00:12:53.022 --> 00:12:57.610
The country is already full of failed
or abandoned projects, and I think they

00:12:57.610 --> 00:13:01.989
need to be spending this money
on improving the existing infrastructure

00:13:02.323 --> 00:13:05.868
and improving the lives of the people
who already live there.

00:13:05.951 --> 00:13:09.121
You know, if sustainability
and creating a better future

00:13:09.121 --> 00:13:13.334
really is their goal, what's also missing
from their marketing campaigns

00:13:13.334 --> 00:13:17.755
is that there's thousands of people
already living in this area.

00:13:18.130 --> 00:13:21.217
There's a tribal community
that's been there for centuries,

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:25.679
and Neom is displacing
thousands of tribal members

00:13:25.679 --> 00:13:30.935
and I think that brings us
to the whole ethics behind all this.

00:13:31.060 --> 00:13:35.773
It is true that Mohammed bin
Salman is making a lot of social

00:13:35.773 --> 00:13:39.610
and cultural progress in Saudi Arabia,
especially for women.

00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:42.655
Since 2018, women were allowed to drive.

00:13:43.030 --> 00:13:46.450
In 2019,
they were allowed to apply for passports,

00:13:46.450 --> 00:13:48.994
meaning
they could travel without a male guardian.

00:13:48.994 --> 00:13:52.748
And they're also requiring equal pay
for women, for equal work.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:53.624
And, you know,

00:13:53.624 --> 00:13:56.585
before that, women got about
half of what men were getting paid

00:13:56.585 --> 00:13:58.045
for the same type of work.

00:13:58.045 --> 00:14:02.383
And all of these reforms are a huge step
for women's rights.

00:14:02.424 --> 00:14:05.845
But the truth of the matter is,

00:14:05.845 --> 00:14:09.640
nobody can criticize the prince
or his policies.

00:14:09.765 --> 00:14:14.186
You guys might have heard of the murder
of Jamal Khashoggi, who was a Saudi

00:14:14.186 --> 00:14:18.691
Arabian journalist who was critical
of the prince and its policies.

00:14:18.732 --> 00:14:22.945
He was actually dismembered
and murdered in Istanbul.

00:14:22.945 --> 00:14:26.615
And CIA reports
found that Mohammad bin Salman,

00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:30.494
although he denies
it, is the one who ordered the killing.

00:14:30.619 --> 00:14:34.665
He's also been responsible
for some serious human rights violations,

00:14:34.957 --> 00:14:39.044
including the arrest of a massive number
of political activists

00:14:39.044 --> 00:14:41.589
throughout the country
in this project as well.

00:14:41.630 --> 00:14:44.466
There's been zero consultation of people

00:14:44.675 --> 00:14:48.220
who are going to live there
or who live there already.

00:14:48.345 --> 00:14:51.432
And so, I don't know, maybe the purpose

00:14:51.432 --> 00:14:54.476
of this whole project
isn't to build a real city.

00:14:54.518 --> 00:14:55.269
After all.

00:14:55.269 --> 00:14:59.857
Maybe the whole purpose of this project
is to repair the reputation

00:14:59.857 --> 00:15:04.111
and promote this positive image
for the prince and his kingdom.

00:15:04.153 --> 00:15:08.449
And they're saying to international
investors, look, women can drive now.

00:15:08.741 --> 00:15:10.367
You can wear what you want.

00:15:10.367 --> 00:15:12.119
We're open for business.

00:15:12.119 --> 00:15:15.122
I don't want to get too bogged down
into the politics here,

00:15:15.122 --> 00:15:18.250
but I think this is an interesting point
to end the video.

00:15:18.459 --> 00:15:21.879
Architecture has been used
throughout history to promote

00:15:21.879 --> 00:15:27.051
a political agenda
and the simpler the idea, the better,

00:15:27.134 --> 00:15:32.306
especially today with political messaging
being delivered right to our fingertips

00:15:32.556 --> 00:15:37.102
as beautiful images
and these grand utopian visions.

00:15:37.478 --> 00:15:40.356
It's really hard to distinguish
what's what.

00:15:40.397 --> 00:15:43.317
It really makes me wonder
what is our role in this?

00:15:43.359 --> 00:15:48.072
I remember taking a course on ethics
in architecture and this architect,

00:15:48.072 --> 00:15:52.826
Carl Sappers, he outlined
the ethical obligations of an architect.

00:15:52.868 --> 00:15:57.456
The architect has the obligation
to, number one, make sufficient

00:15:57.456 --> 00:16:01.335
income to support the architect
and his or her family.

00:16:01.377 --> 00:16:04.964
Number two,
he has the responsibility to the

00:16:04.964 --> 00:16:08.884
sometimes conflicting requirements
of serving the client.

00:16:08.968 --> 00:16:12.429
And number three, to the
sometimes preceding

00:16:12.429 --> 00:16:15.766
and competing interest
of serving the public.

00:16:15.808 --> 00:16:18.852
And number four, he has the responsibility

00:16:18.852 --> 00:16:22.022
to have devotion
to the art of architecture.

00:16:22.064 --> 00:16:24.858
The second and third are actually outlined
in the AA

00:16:24.858 --> 00:16:29.113
or ABC Code of Ethics,
and not all of these points are equal.

00:16:29.196 --> 00:16:31.865
If there is a conflict
between any of these points,

00:16:31.865 --> 00:16:34.785
you have to put the client's
interests over your own,

00:16:34.868 --> 00:16:39.039
and you have to put the public's interests
over yours or the client's.

00:16:39.206 --> 00:16:41.500
What is the architect's responsibility

00:16:41.500 --> 00:16:44.461
when they're asked to come up
with a concept like this anyways?

00:16:44.753 --> 00:16:46.296
I'll leave you guys with that.

00:16:46.296 --> 00:16:48.966
I'm really curious what you think.

00:16:48.966 --> 00:16:51.427
So let me know
your thoughts in the comments.

00:16:51.427 --> 00:16:54.638
If you like this video,
I think you'll also like the video

00:16:54.638 --> 00:16:58.100
where I talk about
architectural concepts in sci fi movies.

00:16:58.392 --> 00:16:59.560
I'll leave it right here.

00:16:59.560 --> 00:17:21.707
And with that,
I'll see you guys in the next video. Bye.

